\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


A quantum LDPC code is specified by commuting checks of bounded weight, with each qubit participating in only boundedly many checks. An asymptotically good family has encoded dimension corresponding to a linear number of logical qubits and minimum distance linear in the number of physical qubits. Local testability requires bounded-size local checks to detect distance from the code with a quantitative soundness guarantee. A probabilistically checkable proof is verified using a bounded number of random proof queries; linear rate asks for proof length linear in the relevant input or witness scale.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Construct asymptotically good, locally testable quantum LDPC codes, and construct probabilistically checkable proofs of linear rate. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2021/54}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The soundness notion for quantum distance and the size parameter for linear-rate PCPs must follow the chosen source model. The supplied sentence states a construction agenda without numerical constants or a particular verifier specification.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Construct quantum codes with simultaneous rate, distance, and local testing guarantees, and proof systems with linear proof length.

